ADDART({id:"luzon",t:"Luzon",c:"Philippines",i:"🏝️",w:"Luzon",r:["manila","mindanao","visayas","banaue","mayon"],
p:[["The largest island","Luzon is the biggest and most populous island of the Philippines."],["Home of the capital","Manila and most of the country’s people and industry are on Luzon."],["Mountains and plains","It has the Cordillera mountains, the Central Luzon rice plains and the Bicol volcanoes."]],
l:"Luzon is the largest and most populous island of the Philippines and the fourth most populous island in the world, with over 60 million people including Metro Manila. It lies in the north of the archipelago and gives its name to one of the country’s three island groups, along with the Visayas and Mindanao. Luzon holds the capital, Manila, the country’s main ports, industries and universities, and the rice-growing plains of Central Luzon, often called the rice bowl of the Philippines. In the north rise the Cordillera mountains, with the Banaue rice terraces and the summer capital of Baguio; in the southeast, the Bicol region is known for the perfectly shaped Mayon Volcano. Luzon faces the Pacific and is struck by many typhoons each year.",
s:[["Geography","Luzon covers about 109,965 square kilometers. The Sierra Madre, the country’s longest mountain range, runs down its eastern side, and the Cordillera Central rises in the north, with Mount Pulag, the island’s highest peak at 2,928 meters. Between them lies the Cagayan Valley. The Central Luzon plain stretches north of Manila, and the volcanic Bicol Peninsula extends to the southeast. Taal Volcano sits in a lake south of Manila."],["People and regions","Luzon includes eight regions: Ilocos, the Cordillera Administrative Region, Cagayan Valley, Central Luzon, the National Capital Region, Calabarzon, Mimaropa and Bicol. Its peoples speak Tagalog, Ilocano, Kapampangan, Pangasinan, Bikol and many other languages, and the Cordillera is home to Indigenous peoples such as the Ifugao, Kalinga and Kankanaey."],["History","Luzon was home to the Tabon and Callao early humans and to trading communities that dealt with China and Japan long before the Spanish arrived. Manila became the Spanish capital in 1571. Luzon saw the start of the Philippine Revolution in 1896, the Bataan Death March and the Battle of Manila in the Second World War, and the eruption of Mount Pinatubo in 1991, one of the largest of the 20th century."]],
k:[["Area","About 109,965 km²"],["Population","Over 60 million"],["Highest peak","Mount Pulag, 2,928 m"],["Capital on the island","Manila"],["Longest range","Sierra Madre"]],
d:[["c.67,000 years ago","Early humans live at Callao Cave"],["1571","Manila becomes the Spanish capital"],["1896","The Philippine Revolution begins in Luzon"],["1942","The Bataan Death March"],["1945","The Battle of Manila"],["1991","Mount Pinatubo erupts"]],
s2:[["The rice bowl","Central Luzon’s flat, well-watered plains produce much of the country’s rice, and the International Rice Research Institute at Los Baños, south of Manila, has developed high-yielding rice varieties grown across Asia since the 1960s."],["Callao Man","In Callao Cave in Cagayan, scientists found bones of a previously unknown human species, Homo luzonensis, at least 50,000 years old, announced in 2019. The small-bodied species lived on Luzon long before modern humans arrived."]],
sp:["Typhoon alley","Luzon’s eastern coast faces the Pacific, where most typhoons that strike the Philippines come from. The Sierra Madre mountains act as a barrier, weakening storms before they reach the populous plains, which is one reason conservationists want to protect its forests."],
g:[["Archipelago","A group of islands"],["Cordillera","A long chain of mountains"],["Region","An administrative division of the Philippines"],["Rice bowl","An area that grows a large share of a country’s rice"]],
f:["Luzon is the fourth most populous island in the world.","Mount Pulag is Luzon’s highest peak.","A new human species, Homo luzonensis, was found on Luzon."],
fun:"Luzon is larger than Cuba, Iceland or South Korea."});

ADDART({id:"visayas",t:"The Visayas",c:"Philippines",i:"🏖️",w:"Visayas",r:["cebu","bohol","boracay","lapulapu","magellan"],
p:[["The central islands","The Visayas are the islands in the middle of the Philippines."],["Islands and seas","Major islands include Cebu, Panay, Negros, Leyte, Samar and Bohol."],["Where Magellan landed","Magellan reached the Visayas in 1521 and died at Mactan."]],
l:"The Visayas are the central island group of the Philippines, between Luzon to the north and Mindanao to the south, made up of thousands of islands around the Visayan Sea. The largest are Panay, Negros, Cebu, Bohol, Leyte and Samar. About 20 million people live there, speaking Visayan languages such as Cebuano, Hiligaynon and Waray. The Visayas were the first part of the Philippines reached by Spanish expeditions: Ferdinand Magellan landed in 1521, the first Catholic mass in the islands was held on Limasawa, and Lapulapu defeated Magellan at Mactan. Cebu City, founded in 1565, is the country’s oldest Spanish city and the center of the Visayas. The islands are known for beaches such as Boracay, the Chocolate Hills, festivals such as Sinulog and Ati-Atihan, and rich marine life.",
s:[["Islands and regions","The Visayas are divided into Western Visayas, with Panay and Guimaras and the city of Iloilo; Central Visayas, with Cebu and Bohol; and Eastern Visayas, with Leyte and Samar. Negros Island, famous for sugar, is split between the west and central regions. The islands are mostly hilly, with narrow coastal plains and many coral reefs."],["Peoples and languages","Visayan languages are spoken by more Filipinos than any other group: Cebuano across the central and southern islands, Hiligaynon in Panay and western Negros, and Waray in Leyte and Samar. Before the Spanish, Visayan communities were known for their tattooed warriors, whom the Spanish called pintados, and for their boats and trade."],["History","After Magellan, Miguel López de Legazpi arrived in Cebu in 1565 and founded the first Spanish settlement. In 1944, General Douglas MacArthur landed on Leyte, beginning the liberation of the Philippines, and the Battle of Leyte Gulf was the largest naval battle in history. In 2013, Typhoon Yolanda (Haiyan) devastated Leyte and Samar, killing thousands."]],
k:[["Largest islands","Panay, Negros, Cebu, Bohol, Leyte, Samar"],["Population","About 20 million"],["Main city","Cebu City"],["Main languages","Cebuano, Hiligaynon, Waray"],["Regions","Western, Central, Eastern Visayas"]],
d:[["1521","Magellan lands; first mass; Battle of Mactan"],["1565","Legazpi founds the Spanish settlement at Cebu"],["1898","Visayan revolutionaries rise against Spain"],["1944","MacArthur lands on Leyte"],["2013","Typhoon Yolanda strikes Leyte and Samar"],["2021","500th anniversary of the Battle of Mactan"]],
s2:[["The Sinulog","Every January, Cebu City holds the Sinulog festival in honor of the Santo Niño, the image of the child Jesus given by Magellan’s expedition in 1521. Dancers in bright costumes move two steps forward and one step back to drums, and millions join the celebrations."],["The San Juanico Bridge","The San Juanico Bridge, opened in 1973, links Leyte and Samar across the narrow San Juanico Strait. At about 2.2 kilometers, it was long the country’s longest bridge, and it is famous for its graceful curves."]],
sp:["The Visayan Sea","The shallow seas between the Visayan islands are among the richest fishing grounds in the Philippines and are home to whale sharks, which gather near Donsol and Oslob, sea turtles and the coral gardens of Apo Island, a model of community-based marine conservation."],
g:[["Visayan","Relating to the Visayas or its peoples and languages"],["Pintados","‘Painted ones’, the Spanish name for tattooed Visayan warriors"],["Strait","A narrow stretch of water between two islands"],["Santo Niño","An image of the child Jesus venerated in Cebu"]],
f:["Visayan languages have more speakers than any other Philippine language group.","Magellan died at Mactan in 1521.","The Battle of Leyte Gulf was the largest naval battle in history."],
fun:"Early Spanish visitors called Visayans ‘pintados’ because of their elaborate tattoos."});

ADDART({id:"davao",t:"Davao City",c:"Philippines",i:"🌳",w:"Davao City",r:["mindanao","apo","phfood","phbio","indig"],
p:[["Mindanao’s largest city","Davao City is the biggest city in Mindanao and one of the largest in the Philippines."],["Beneath Mount Apo","The country’s highest mountain rises just west of the city."],["Durian and eagles","Davao is known for durian fruit and the Philippine Eagle Center."]],
l:"Davao City is the largest city in Mindanao and the third most populous city in the Philippines, with about 1.8 million people. It lies on the Davao Gulf in southeastern Mindanao, at the foot of Mount Apo, the country’s highest mountain. By land area, it is one of the largest cities in the world, covering about 2,444 square kilometers, most of it farmland and forest. Davao is the commercial and government center of southern Mindanao, a major port for bananas, pineapples, coconuts and durian, and home to the Philippine Eagle Center, which breeds the endangered national bird. Its people include Lumad Indigenous groups such as the Bagobo, Muslim communities and migrants from the Visayas and Luzon. The Kadayawan Festival each August celebrates the harvest and the city’s cultures.",
s:[["History","The Davao Gulf area was home to the Bagobo and other Lumad peoples and to Muslim communities. Spain established a settlement in 1848 after a battle with the local Moro leader Datu Bago. Japanese immigrants developed large abaca plantations in the early 1900s, making Davao one of the biggest Japanese communities outside Japan before the Second World War. It became a city in 1936."],["The city today","Davao is a hub of agriculture, trade and services, with a busy port and international airport. It has been known for strict local rules, including a smoking ban and a speed limit, and for a reputation for order that is also linked to criticism of its past approach to crime. Mindanao’s universities, hospitals and media are concentrated there."],["Nature","Mount Apo, 2,954 meters high, rises within the city’s boundaries and those of neighboring provinces. The Philippine Eagle Center in Malagos cares for and breeds the giant Philippine eagle. Nearby Samal Island has beaches and the Monfort Bat Cave, home to millions of fruit bats."]],
k:[["Population","About 1.8 million"],["Land area","About 2,444 km²"],["Region","Davao Region, Mindanao"],["Mountain","Mount Apo, 2,954 m"],["Festival","Kadayawan, August"]],
d:[["1848","Spanish settlement after the defeat of Datu Bago"],["1900s","Japanese abaca plantations grow"],["1936","Davao becomes a city"],["1942–45","Japanese occupation"],["1988","Kadayawan Festival takes its current form"],["2016","A Davao mayor becomes president of the Philippines"]],
s2:[["Durian","Davao is famous for durian, the strong-smelling fruit with a custard-like flesh that people either love or hate. The city celebrates it with durian candies, ice cream and a durian monument, and warns visitors that the fruit is banned in many hotels because of its smell."],["The Philippine eagle","The Philippine eagle, one of the largest and rarest eagles in the world, nests in Mindanao’s forests. The Philippine Eagle Center near Davao hatched the first captive-bred eagle, Pag-asa, meaning ‘hope’, in 1992, and continues breeding and releasing birds."]],
sp:["Kadayawan","Kadayawan, from a Mandaya word meaning something good or valuable, is Davao’s thanksgiving festival for the harvest and the city’s Indigenous cultures. It features street dancing by groups representing the city’s eleven tribes, floral floats and displays of fruit and flowers."],
g:[["Lumad","Indigenous peoples of Mindanao"],["Abaca","A plant whose fibers are used for rope and cloth"],["Gulf","A large bay"],["Datu","A traditional chief"]],
f:["Davao City is one of the largest cities in the world by area.","Mount Apo lies partly within the city.","Davao was home to a large Japanese community before the Second World War."],
fun:"Durian is so smelly that it is banned from many hotels and buses in Davao, yet the city proudly calls itself the Durian Capital."});

ADDART({id:"iloilo",t:"Iloilo City",c:"Philippines",i:"🏛️",w:"Iloilo City",r:["visayas","phfood","phfest","spanph","bahaynabato"],
p:[["Queen City of the South","Iloilo City is the capital of Iloilo province and the center of Western Visayas."],["Heritage and food","It is known for Spanish-era mansions, old churches and dishes such as La Paz batchoy."],["Festival city","The Dinagyang festival each January fills its streets with dancers."]],
l:"Iloilo City is a city on the southern coast of Panay Island and the regional center of Western Visayas, with about 460,000 people. It grew rich in the 19th century as a port exporting sugar from Panay and Negros and became known as the ‘Queen City of the South’. Its districts of Jaro, Molo, La Paz, Mandurriao, Arevalo and the City Proper preserve churches, plazas and grand houses from the Spanish and American periods; the Calle Real heritage street has been restored. Iloilo is famous for its food, including La Paz batchoy, a noodle soup, and pancit Molo, and for the Dinagyang festival honoring the Santo Niño. It was named a UNESCO Creative City of Gastronomy in 2023, and its Esplanade along the Iloilo River is a model of urban renewal.",
s:[["History","Iloilo was a center of textile weaving before the Spanish, and Jaro became the seat of a bishop in 1865. When the port opened to international trade in 1855, sugar exports brought wealth, mansions and the first commercial bank outside Manila. In 1898, the Spanish surrendered Iloilo to Filipino revolutionaries, who flew the Philippine flag there in December."],["Heritage","Jaro Cathedral, Molo Church, with its all-female array of saints, and the Miagao Church nearby, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, are among the Visayas’ finest churches. Calle Real, the old commercial street, has restored Art Deco and neoclassical buildings. Many ancestral houses survive in Jaro and Molo."],["Iloilo today","The city has grown into a center of business process outsourcing, education and medical services, with a convention center and new districts. The river Esplanade, bicycle lanes and a cleaned-up Iloilo River have won national and international awards. Guimaras Island, famous for its sweet mangoes, lies across the strait."]],
k:[["Island","Panay"],["Population","About 460,000"],["Region","Western Visayas (center)"],["Festival","Dinagyang, January"],["UNESCO title","Creative City of Gastronomy, 2023"]],
d:[["1855","The port opens to international trade"],["1865","Jaro becomes a diocese"],["1898","Spanish forces surrender Iloilo"],["1937","Iloilo becomes a chartered city"],["1968","The first Dinagyang festival"],["2023","Named a UNESCO Creative City of Gastronomy"]],
s2:[["La Paz batchoy","La Paz batchoy, created in the La Paz public market in the 1930s, is a soup of egg noodles in rich pork broth topped with pork, liver, crushed pork cracklings, spring onions and sometimes a raw egg. It is Iloilo’s signature dish and is sold across the country."],["Dinagyang","Dinagyang, held on the fourth weekend of January, honors the Santo Niño and the arrival of Malay settlers in Panay according to legend. ‘Tribes’ of dancers with painted bodies and towering costumes perform choreographed routines to drums, competing for prizes before huge crowds."]],
sp:["Miagao Church","The Church of Santo Tomás de Villanueva in Miagao, south of the city, was completed in 1797 as both a church and a fortress against raids. Its facade is carved with a coconut tree, papaya and guava plants, blending Christian images with Philippine nature. It is one of four Baroque churches of the Philippines listed by UNESCO."],
g:[["Esplanade","A long open walkway, often along water"],["Heritage","Buildings, traditions and objects passed down from the past"],["Gastronomy","The art and culture of good food"],["Chartered city","A city with its own charter and government"]],
f:["Iloilo’s port opened to world trade in 1855.","La Paz batchoy was created in a public market in the 1930s.","Miagao Church was built as a fortress against raids."],
fun:"Molo Church is nicknamed the ‘feminist church’ because all 16 statues of saints inside are women."});

ADDART({id:"barangay",t:"The Barangay",c:"Philippines",i:"🏘️",w:"Barangay",r:["phgov","precol","democ","ofw","manila"],
p:[["The smallest unit of government","A barangay is a village, district or neighborhood, the basic unit of local government in the Philippines."],["An ancient word","The name comes from the balangay, the boats in which early settlers arrived."],["Over 42,000 barangays","Every Filipino lives in one, from tiny villages to crowded city districts."]],
l:"The barangay is the smallest unit of government in the Philippines, equivalent to a village, district or ward. There are more than 42,000 barangays, ranging from remote mountain villages to urban neighborhoods with tens of thousands of residents. Each is led by an elected barangay captain, the punong barangay, and a council of seven kagawad, along with a youth council, the Sangguniang Kabataan. Barangays handle local matters such as settling disputes, keeping peace, running health centers and day-care centers, maintaining roads and issuing clearances. The word comes from balangay, the large wooden boats in which Austronesian settlers arrived, and before Spanish rule a barangay was a community of families led by a datu.",
s:[["Before the Spanish","Early Philippine communities were small, usually 30 to 100 families, led by a datu who settled disputes, led in war and received a share of harvests. These communities were often located along rivers and coasts and traded with each other and with China and Borneo. The Spanish kept the name and made barangays the lowest level of their colonial administration."],["Barrio to barangay","Under Spanish and American rule, the units were called barrios, headed by a cabeza de barangay and later a teniente del barrio. In 1974, under President Ferdinand Marcos, barrios were renamed barangays, and in 1991 the Local Government Code gave barangays their present powers and a share of national taxes."],["What a barangay does","The barangay hall is where residents get clearances, register complaints and attend assemblies. The Lupong Tagapamayapa, a peace council, settles minor disputes between neighbors before they reach the courts. Barangay health workers run clinics and vaccination drives, tanods patrol as volunteer watchmen, and during typhoons and floods the barangay organizes evacuation and relief."]],
k:[["Number","More than 42,000"],["Leader","Punong barangay (captain)"],["Council","Seven kagawad"],["Youth council","Sangguniang Kabataan"],["Word origin","Balangay, a boat"]],
d:[["Before 1521","Communities led by datus"],["1500s","Spanish rule keeps the barangay as the lowest unit"],["1900s","Units known as barrios"],["1974","Barrios renamed barangays"],["1991","The Local Government Code sets present powers"],["2023","Barangay and youth council elections held nationwide"]],
s2:[["The balangay boats","In 1976, ancient wooden boats were excavated at Butuan in Mindanao, the oldest dated to around AD 320. These balangays, up to 15 meters long and built of planks joined with pegs and fiber, carried people and goods across the seas. Replicas have sailed around Southeast Asia."],["Barangay elections","Barangay officials are elected by residents, and the elections are non-partisan: candidates do not run under political parties. Tens of thousands of positions are filled on a single day, making them among the largest local elections in the world."]],
sp:["Bayanihan","Many barangays keep alive the spirit of bayanihan, neighbors helping each other, from the famous image of villagers carrying a bamboo house on their shoulders to modern clean-up drives, feeding programs and relief operations after disasters."],
g:[["Datu","A traditional chief"],["Kagawad","A barangay council member"],["Tanod","A volunteer barangay watchman"],["Clearance","An official document certifying a resident’s record"]],
f:["The Philippines has more than 42,000 barangays.","The word barangay comes from a type of boat.","Barangay elections are held without political parties."],
fun:"The oldest balangay boat found at Butuan is about 1,700 years old."});

ADDART({id:"phflag",t:"The Philippine Flag and National Anthem",c:"Philippines",i:"🇵🇭",w:"Flag of the Philippines",r:["independence","aguinaldo","phrev","phgov","phlang"],
p:[["Sun, stars and stripes","The flag has a white triangle with a sun and three stars, and blue and red stripes."],["First raised in 1898","It was unfurled at the declaration of independence in Kawit, Cavite."],["Lupang Hinirang","The anthem’s music was composed in 1898 and its Tagalog words adopted in 1956."]],
l:"The national flag of the Philippines has a horizontal blue stripe above a red stripe, with a white equilateral triangle at the hoist containing a golden sun with eight rays and three five-pointed stars. The sun stands for liberty and its rays for the first eight provinces that rose against Spain in 1896; the three stars represent Luzon, the Visayas and Mindanao; white stands for equality, blue for peace and justice, and red for patriotism and valor. Uniquely, the flag is flown with the red stripe on top in time of war. It was designed by Emilio Aguinaldo, sewn in Hong Kong by Marcela Agoncillo, her daughter Lorenza and Delfina Herbosa de Natividad, and first unfurled on 12 June 1898 at the declaration of independence in Kawit, Cavite. The national anthem, Lupang Hinirang, was composed by Julián Felipe for the same ceremony.",
s:[["The making of the flag","While in exile in Hong Kong in 1898, Aguinaldo sketched the design, and Marcela Agoncillo, wife of a revolutionary diplomat, sewed it by hand in five days with her daughter and a niece of José Rizal. The flag was brought to Cavite and raised from a window of Aguinaldo’s house as the independence declaration was read."],["Banned and restored","Under American rule, the flag was banned from 1907 to 1919 as a symbol of rebellion. The shade of blue has changed several times; since 1998, the law specifies royal blue. The Flag and Heraldic Code of 1998 sets rules for display, including that the flag must never touch the ground and that the red field is placed on top only in war."],["The anthem","Julián Felipe composed the march, then called Marcha Nacional Filipina, and it was played by a band at Kawit on 12 June 1898. Spanish words were written in 1899 by José Palma. English versions followed under American rule, and the official Tagalog lyrics, Lupang Hinirang, ‘Chosen Land’, were adopted in 1956. The law requires the anthem to be sung with the original march tempo."]],
k:[["First raised","12 June 1898, Kawit, Cavite"],["Designer","Emilio Aguinaldo"],["Sewn by","Marcela Agoncillo and companions"],["Anthem composer","Julián Felipe"],["Tagalog lyrics adopted","1956"]],
d:[["1896","The revolution begins; eight provinces rise"],["1898","Flag sewn in Hong Kong and raised at Kawit"],["1899","Spanish lyrics written for the anthem"],["1907–19","The flag is banned under American rule"],["1956","Lupang Hinirang’s Tagalog lyrics adopted"],["1998","The Flag and Heraldic Code is passed"]],
s2:[["The eight rays","The eight rays of the sun stand for the first provinces placed under martial law by Spain in 1896 for rising in revolt: Manila, Cavite, Bulacan, Pampanga, Nueva Ecija, Tarlac, Laguna and Batangas. Proposals have been made to add a ninth ray for the Muslim peoples of Mindanao who resisted colonial rule."],["Flag Day","National Flag Day is celebrated on 28 May, the anniversary of the Battle of Alapan in 1898, when the flag was first flown in battle, and the flag is displayed everywhere until Independence Day on 12 June."]],
sp:["Flying the flag","The flag is raised briskly and lowered slowly, flown from sunrise to sunset unless lit at night, and, when worn out, burned respectfully rather than thrown away. On the flag of the Philippines alone among national flags, a change in orientation, red on top, signals a state of war."],
g:[["Hoist","The side of a flag nearest the pole"],["Anthem","A country’s official song"],["Heraldic code","Laws about flags, seals and symbols"],["Tempo","The speed of a piece of music"]],
f:["The flag is flown with red on top in time of war.","It was sewn by hand in Hong Kong in five days.","The anthem was first played without words in 1898."],
fun:"The Philippine flag is the only national flag that changes meaning when flipped upside down."});

ADDART({id:"gdelpilar",t:"Gregorio del Pilar",c:"People",i:"⚔️",w:"Gregorio del Pilar",r:["phamwar","aguinaldo","aluna","delpilar","phrev"],
p:[["The boy general","Gregorio del Pilar became a general in the Philippine revolutionary army at 22."],["Tirad Pass","He died in 1899 defending a mountain pass to let Aguinaldo escape."],["A national hero","His stand is remembered as a Philippine Thermopylae."]],
l:"Gregorio del Pilar was a Filipino general in the Philippine Revolution and the Philippine–American War, one of the youngest generals in the revolutionary army and one of its most celebrated heroes. Born in Bulacan in 1875, a nephew of the reformist writer Marcelo H. del Pilar, he joined the revolution against Spain in 1896 and rose rapidly, becoming a general at 22. During the war against the United States, he commanded the rear guard protecting President Emilio Aguinaldo’s retreat into the mountains of northern Luzon. On 2 December 1899, he and about 60 men held Tirad Pass in Ilocos Sur against some 300 American soldiers for hours before he was killed. His stand gave Aguinaldo time to escape and made him a symbol of youthful courage.",
s:[["Early life","Del Pilar was born in Bulakan, Bulacan, on 14 November 1875, into a family of reformists; his uncle Marcelo edited La Solidaridad. He studied at the Ateneo de Manila and joined the Katipunan uprising in 1896, fighting in Bulacan. He went into exile in Hong Kong with Aguinaldo in 1897 and returned with him in 1898."],["General at 22","After the declaration of independence in 1898, del Pilar led forces that captured Bulacan and Nueva Ecija from the Spanish, and Aguinaldo promoted him to brigadier general. Handsome and dashing, he was known as the ‘boy general’ and became one of Aguinaldo’s most trusted officers."],["Tirad Pass","Pursued by American troops in late 1899, Aguinaldo fled north. Del Pilar chose to defend Tirad Pass, a narrow trail about 1,400 meters high, with a small force. His men held off the Americans until a local guide led the attackers around the position. Del Pilar was shot and killed; an American officer later found his diary, in which he had written that he was resolved to fall for his country."]],
k:[["Born","14 November 1875, Bulakan, Bulacan"],["Died","2 December 1899, Tirad Pass"],["Rank","Brigadier general, at 22"],["Famous battle","Tirad Pass"],["Uncle","Marcelo H. del Pilar"]],
d:[["1875","Born in Bulacan"],["1896","Joins the revolution"],["1898","Returns from Hong Kong; made a general"],["1899","Commands Aguinaldo’s rear guard"],["2 Dec 1899","Killed at Tirad Pass"],["1930s","Honored with monuments; a town is named after him"]],
s2:[["The diary","Before the battle, del Pilar wrote in his diary that the general had given him a picked body of men and that he was resolved to fall at the pass rather than let the enemy pass, as he realized the terrible odds. The entry, found after his death, became one of the most quoted passages in Philippine history."],["Remembering Tirad Pass","Tirad Pass is a national shrine, and 2 December is observed in his memory. The town of Gregorio del Pilar in Ilocos Sur, a fort in Baguio that houses the Philippine Military Academy and schools around the country bear his name."]],
sp:["A Philippine Thermopylae","The stand at Tirad Pass is often compared with the Battle of Thermopylae in 480 BC, where a small Greek force led by King Leonidas of Sparta held a mountain pass against a huge Persian army until betrayed by a local who showed the Persians a way around."],
g:[["Rear guard","Troops protecting the back of a retreating army"],["Pass","A route through mountains"],["Brigadier general","A junior general’s rank"],["Shrine","A place honored for its history or sacredness"]],
f:["Del Pilar became a general at 22.","He held Tirad Pass with about 60 men.","He died at 24."],
fun:"Fort del Pilar in Baguio, home of the Philippine Military Academy, is named after him."});

ADDART({id:"romulo",t:"Carlos P. Romulo",c:"People",i:"🌐",w:"Carlos P. Romulo",r:["un","commonwealth","pacificwar","journalism","phgov"],
p:[["Diplomat and writer","Carlos P. Romulo was a Filipino journalist, soldier and diplomat."],["Voice at the United Nations","He signed the UN Charter and became the first Asian president of the UN General Assembly."],["Pulitzer Prize","He won the prize in 1942 for his reporting on the coming war in Asia."]],
l:"Carlos P. Romulo was a Filipino journalist, author, soldier and diplomat who represented the Philippines on the world stage for half a century. Born in Camiling, Tarlac, in 1898, he became a newspaper editor and in 1942 won the Pulitzer Prize for articles warning of war in the Pacific. During the Second World War he served on General MacArthur’s staff, broadcast as the ‘Voice of Freedom’ from Corregidor and rose to the rank of brigadier general. In 1945, he signed the United Nations Charter for the Philippines, and in 1949 he became the first Asian to serve as president of the UN General Assembly. He later served as ambassador to the United States, president of the University of the Philippines and foreign secretary for almost two decades.",
s:[["Journalist","Romulo edited newspapers in Manila from the 1920s and became publisher of a chain of papers. In 1941, he toured Asia and wrote a series of articles predicting that Japan would strike and that Asian peoples would demand independence; they won him the 1942 Pulitzer Prize for correspondence, the first awarded to an Asian."],["War and the United Nations","After Japan invaded, Romulo broadcast daily messages of resistance from Corregidor and was among the last to leave before it fell. He joined the government in exile and returned with MacArthur in 1944. At the San Francisco conference of 1945, he argued forcefully for the independence of colonized peoples and signed the UN Charter."],["Statesman","As president of the UN General Assembly in 1949–50, he presided over debates in the early Cold War. He served as the Philippines’ ambassador in Washington, president of the University of the Philippines from 1962 to 1968 and secretary of foreign affairs from 1968 to 1984, helping found ASEAN. He wrote many books, including ‘I Walked with Heroes’, and was named a National Artist for Literature in 1982."]],
k:[["Born","14 January 1898, Camiling, Tarlac"],["Died","15 December 1985"],["Pulitzer Prize","1942"],["UN General Assembly president","1949–50"],["Foreign secretary","1968–84"]],
d:[["1898","Born in Tarlac"],["1942","Wins the Pulitzer Prize; broadcasts from Corregidor"],["1945","Signs the United Nations Charter"],["1949","Elected president of the UN General Assembly"],["1962","Becomes president of the University of the Philippines"],["1982","Named National Artist for Literature"]],
s2:[["The small man who spoke loudly","Romulo was short, about 1.63 meters, and joked about it. At the UN, he once answered a tall Soviet delegate who called him a little man from a little country by saying it was the duty of little Davids to fling pebbles of truth at the eyes of blustering Goliaths. He was known for his wit and his powerful speeches."],["The Voice of Freedom","From the tunnels of Corregidor in early 1942, Romulo broadcast nightly to Filipinos under Japanese occupation, reporting the defense of Bataan and urging them not to lose hope. He left the island by plane on the orders of MacArthur shortly before Bataan fell."]],
sp:["Romulo and ASEAN","As foreign secretary, Romulo was one of the signatories who founded the Association of Southeast Asian Nations in Bangkok in 1967, and he worked to make the Philippines a bridge between Asia and the West."],
g:[["Diplomat","An official who represents a country abroad"],["Charter","A founding document setting out rules and aims"],["General Assembly","The main meeting of all UN member states"],["Correspondent","A journalist who reports from a particular place"]],
f:["Romulo was the first Asian to win a Pulitzer Prize.","He signed the United Nations Charter in 1945.","He served as foreign secretary for about 16 years."],
fun:"Romulo joked that he was the only general in the US Army who had never fired a shot."});

